-- title:
--   Failure of stalk ideal containment at a specialisation point
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$, $J$ be ideal sheaf data on $X$ (Mathlib's `IdealSheafData`: a compatible assignment of an ideal $I(U) \subseteq \mathcal{O}_X(U)$ to each affine open $U$), let $U$ be an affine open of $X$, and let $x, \xi$ be points of $X$ with $x \in U$. Assume $\xi \rightsquigarrow x$, that is, $\xi$ specialises to $x$ (so $\xi$ is a generisation of $x$), and assume $\xi \notin \operatorname{Supp} I$ while $\xi \in \operatorname{Supp} J$, where the supports are the closed sets attached to the ideal sheaf data. The conclusion is a negation: the ideal of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ generated by the image of $I(U)$ under the germ map $\mathcal{O}_X(U) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ is *not* contained in the ideal generated by the image of $J(U)$ under the same germ map. In symbols, $I(U)\,\mathcal{O}_{X,x} \not\subseteq J(U)\,\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$.
--
--   This is the local criterion separating two ideal sheaves at a point by testing at a generisation: no containment of stalk ideals at $x$ can hold once some generisation of $x$ lies off the support of the smaller sheaf but on the support of the larger one. On an arithmetic surface it says that the stalk ideal at a special point of a subscheme with no vertical component is contained in no branch ideal; it is used in the analysis of ideals on a two-chart model of $X_1(p)$, towards local principality statements.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_not_map_germ_ideal_le_of_specializes_of_notMem_support_of_mem_support.lean

import Mathlib

